{"kind":"artifact","text":"Candidate at docs for the fan-out contract-full verdict, exact head 0f4ab4c30f414827c1461cbc6aa03ed90376eb8f.\n\nGoverned by request e1ab6474 through promise c5c7aa17. The commit carries Rests-On for e1ab6474 and for e6fcd142, because the same head also retains the evidence the page is derived from.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. docs/reference/performance.md only. It answers every clause of adopted proposal e5b88d52 and the conditions of e1ab6474 in a table, misses included, reports each width in both relative and millisecond terms, and re-anchors onto the live artifacts for the behaviour it describes: cmd/gitseq-perf f0047ba0, internal/perflane 86288a0f and performance 4da5ebd0, all at merge de9a2b83.\n\nTHE RUN. The page states both harness_commit and candidate_commit as f71b4d73c46b5df1ba2c755571fc8ffdf4455275, which I read from evidence.json rather than assuming the two agreed; f71b4d73 is this head's parent. Contract digest b0795bc71c9485210a842decce2fc932627a88072bbd3494a22098fcf66c7d45, which I recomputed through perflane.CorrectnessDigest from performance/contract-v2.json, a file byte-identical to main's by blob id. One fixture exact digest, d15f714d, across all samples. 505 records: 100 primary per width at 1, 8, 16, 64 and 256, plus one round-zero diagnostic each. All 500 primary trusted-versus-projected digest pairs equal, checked by me.\n\nCOSTS PER WIDTH against the width-one p50 of 471.761 ms: -7.292 ms and -1.546 percent at width 8, -32.199 ms and -6.825 percent at 16, -28.573 ms and -6.057 percent at 64, and -29.246 ms and -6.199 percent at 256. Nothing clamped, no magnitudes, no cross-case pairing. The only increase between adjacent widths is +3.626 ms from 16 to 64; the largest step in absolute terms is the fall of -24.907 ms from 8 to 16.\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR, AND WHAT THE PAGE DOES NOT CLAIM. Width one runs first and is the axis's sole temporal denominator, and it moved across its own block: quarter medians 448.474, 463.870, 622.700 and 735.251 ms, 36 samples above 600 ms in rounds 46 to 93, where no other width has one and the largest sample outside width one is 598.181 ms. Width eight moved the other way, 496.527 down to 440.960 ms. Machine load was also observed rising during the run. The page does not assert that either fact caused the other. Load was observed only at twenty-second intervals and the latency records carry no wall-clock timestamp, so no sample can be aligned to the machine's state when it was taken; the quarter table is reported as observed and the attribution is left open. Each block's movement is given as one statistic applied identically to all five widths, the range of its four quarter medians as a percentage of that width's own p50: 60.789, 11.964, 1.184, 2.343 and 0.760 percent.\n\nRather than choose a denominator, the page classifies against four of them, the full-block p50, the full-block simple median, and width one's lowest and highest quarters. PREVIEW and FIRST-PRODUCTION pass on all four. Across the four tested denominators the largest observed relative increment is +3.566 percent at width 8; both classifications pass, but the magnitudes remain load- and order-sensitive.\n\nThe page records that this is the second complete campaign on this axis, the first having been suspended by machine sleep partway through width one and discarded whole rather than partly repaired, since a spanning suspend cannot be found afterwards in records that carry no wall-clock timestamp. Both campaigns classified as pass on both envelopes, so neither classification moved and the contract's inconclusiveness rule did not trigger.\n\nMISSES, REPORTED NOT HIDDEN. The fixed one-base append is about nine times its separate 50 ms budget, named to parked request 8add6909 and not re-argued. The page refuses two implications: that a passing ratio says anything about append latency overall, and that a depth-1,000 result is a cross-product result at depth 50,000 or 500,000. The 10 percent allowance is worth between 44.847 and 73.525 ms at this run's denominators, at or above the whole absolute budget, which is why the two targets stay apart.\n\nEVIDENCE THE READER CAN CHECK. The page publishes the three retained files with their SHA-256 digests, each recomputed from the file rather than transcribed, and links them: evidence.json 61a53e96..., samples.jsonl 21c74a93..., candidate.bench f5078a0e.... evidence.json retains the harness's own latency distributions and fan-out axis summary alongside the contract, environment and digests; candidate.bench holds the 500 primary samples in Go benchmark form. No separate derived file is retained, and the page says so accurately rather than claiming nothing derived exists anywhere.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD, each tool's own exit code captured directly: gofmt 0 unformatted, go build 0, go vet 0, go test -count=1 ./... 0 across 26 packages with zero failures, docset uncached at 62.137 seconds. Tree clean; local and origin both at this commit; merge-base with origin/main is 92dd2df1 itself.\n\nPredecessor at this path is fa35d881, live, not stale and not describes_superseded_world when I checked immediately before publishing. Retirement belongs to the merge, not here. I have not merged.","body":{"commit":"0f4ab4c30f414827c1461cbc6aa03ed90376eb8f","path":"docs"}}